{"schemaVersion":1,"workId":"OL8193416W","title":"The Picture of Dorian Gray","authors":["Oscar Wilde"],"firstPublished":1890,"edition":{"description":"Sections as detected in Project Gutenberg eBook #174.","source":"Project Gutenberg eBook #174","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/174"},"sectionLabel":{"singular":"Chapter","plural":"Chapters"},"sections":[{"id":"s1","label":"Ch. 1"},{"id":"s2","label":"Ch. 2"},{"id":"s3","label":"Ch. 3","title":"At half-past twelve next day Lord Henry Wotton strolled from Curzon"},{"id":"s4","label":"Ch. 4"},{"id":"s5","label":"Ch. 5","title":"“Mother, Mother, I am so happy!” whispered the girl, burying her face"},{"id":"s6","label":"Ch. 6","title":"“I suppose you have heard the news, Basil?” said Lord Henry that"},{"id":"s7","label":"Ch. 7"},{"id":"s8","label":"Ch. 8"},{"id":"s9","label":"Ch. 9","title":"As he was sitting at breakfast next morning, Basil Hallward was shown"},{"id":"s10","label":"Ch. 10"},{"id":"s11","label":"Ch. 11","title":"For years, Dorian Gray could not free himself from the influence of"},{"id":"s12","label":"Ch. 12","title":"It was on the ninth of November, the eve of his own thirty-eighth"},{"id":"s13","label":"Ch. 13","title":"He passed out of the room and began the ascent, Basil Hallward"},{"id":"s14","label":"Ch. 14","title":"At nine o’clock the next morning his servant came in with a cup of"},{"id":"s15","label":"Ch. 15"},{"id":"s16","label":"Ch. 16"},{"id":"s17","label":"Ch. 17","title":"A week later Dorian Gray was sitting in the conservatory at Selby"},{"id":"s18","label":"Ch. 18"},{"id":"s19","label":"Ch. 19"},{"id":"s20","label":"Ch. 20"}],"groups":[{"id":"g-vane","label":"Vane (shared surname)"}],"characters":[{"id":"dorian-gray","name":"Dorian Gray","aliases":["Dorian","Mr. Gray","Mr. Dorian Gray"],"role":"protagonist","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: “Your rank and wealth, Harry; my brains, such as they are—my art, whatever it may be worth; Dorian Gray’s good looks—we shall all suffer for what the gods have given us, suffer terribly.””"},{"id":"lord-henry-wotton","name":"Lord Henry","aliases":["Lord Henry Wotton","Henry"],"role":"major","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: “From the corner of the divan of Persian saddle-bags on which he was lying, smoking, as was his custom, innumerable cigarettes, Lord Henry Wotton could just catch the gleam of the honey-sweet and honey-coloured blossom…”"},{"id":"basil-hallward","name":"Basil Hallward","aliases":["Basil","Hallward"],"role":"major","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: “In the centre of the room, clamped to an upright easel, stood the full-length portrait of a young man of extraordinary personal beauty, and in front of it, some little distance away, was sitting the artist himself, Ba…”"},{"id":"harry","name":"Harry","role":"major","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: ““You don’t understand me, Harry,” answered the artist.”"},{"id":"sibyl-vane","name":"Sibyl Vane","aliases":["Sibyl"],"role":"supporting","group":"g-vane","firstAppearance":"s4","summary":"First mention: ““Her name is Sibyl Vane.””"},{"id":"alan-campbell","name":"Alan Campbell","aliases":["Alan","Campbell"],"role":"supporting","firstAppearance":"s13","summary":"First mention: ““Alan Campbell, 152, Hertford Street, Mayfair.”"},{"id":"lady-narborough","name":"Lady Narborough","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s15","summary":"First mention: “That evening, at eight-thirty, exquisitely dressed and wearing a large button-hole of Parma violets, Dorian Gray was ushered into Lady Narborough’s drawing-room by bowing servants.”"},{"id":"james-vane","name":"James Vane","aliases":["James"],"role":"minor","group":"g-vane","firstAppearance":"s5","summary":"First mention: “Isaacs has advanced us fifty pounds to pay off our debts and to get a proper outfit for James.”"},{"id":"jim","name":"Jim","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s5","summary":"First mention: “but you don’t like being kissed, Jim,” she cried.”"},{"id":"mr-erskine","name":"Mr. Erskine","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s3","summary":"First mention: “The post on her left was occupied by Mr.”"},{"id":"prince-charming","name":"Prince Charming","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s4","summary":"First mention: “I must call you Prince Charming.’””"},{"id":"juliet","name":"Juliet","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s4","summary":"First mention: “It was Romeo and Juliet.”"},{"id":"lady-agatha","name":"Lady Agatha","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: “It was at my aunt, Lady Agatha’s.”"},{"id":"gladys","name":"Gladys","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s17","summary":"First mention: ““I hope Dorian has told you about my plan for rechristening everything, Gladys.”"},{"id":"uncle-george","name":"Uncle George","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s3","summary":"First mention: ““Pure family affection, I assure you, Uncle George.”"},{"id":"francis","name":"Francis","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s13","summary":"First mention: ““I am sorry to have had to wake you up, Francis,” he said, stepping in; “but I had forgotten my latch-key.”"},{"id":"duke","name":"Duke","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s1","summary":"First mention: “When we meet—we do meet occasionally, when we dine out together, or go down to the Duke’s—we tell each other the most absurd stories with the most serious faces.”"},{"id":"sir-thomas","name":"Sir Thomas","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s3","summary":"First mention: ““My uncle has already suggested pork-packing, Sir Thomas.””"},{"id":"romeo","name":"Romeo","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s4","summary":"First mention: “It was Romeo and Juliet.”"},{"id":"shakespeare","name":"Shakespeare","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s4","summary":"First mention: “I must admit that I was rather annoyed at the idea of seeing Shakespeare done in such a wretched hole of a place.”"},{"id":"victor","name":"Victor","role":"minor","firstAppearance":"s8","summary":"First mention: “Finally hi